WEBVTT

NOTE This transcript is the episode script as written and fact-checked, timed to the recording. Timestamps are approximate within a sentence.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.700
What's up guys, welcome back to CrimeTimeSnacks.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:15.600
A year after accepting a plea deal to avoid the death penalty, Bryan Kohberger filed a motion to withdraw his guilty plea by claiming he was coerced.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.100
In a handwritten petition, he blamed his plea on threats, false promises, and bad information from his own defense team.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.400
He wants to reopen the case and face a trial.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.900
You've probably heard the headline version of what happened in Moscow, Idaho.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:36.400
In the early morning hours of November 13, 2022, four University of Idaho students were fatally stabbed in their off-campus rental house on King Road.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.000
Ethan Chapin, Madison Mogen, Xana Kernodle, and Kaylee Goncalves were all killed in an attack that lasted roughly fifteen minutes.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.000
The coroner stated they were likely asleep when the intruder entered, though some fought back and had defensive wounds.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.100
Two other roommates were inside the house that night.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.900
The case file shows they remained in the home during the attacks but were left completely unharmed.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.400
When authorities responded to a 911 call just before noon, they found a brutal scene with no signs of forced entry.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.600
They also found a massive piece of evidence left behind on a bed next to one of the victims.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.300
Investigators discovered a leather knife sheath right there at the crime scene.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.500
That sheath yielded touch DNA matching a single male profile.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:24.600
Police used investigative genetic genealogy to link that DNA to public databases, which pointed them straight to a Washington State University criminology student living just eight miles away.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:32.300
On December 30, authorities arrested Bryan Kohberger at his parents' house in Pennsylvania and charged him with four counts of first-degree murder.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.000
He eventually took a plea deal that put him in prison for four consecutive life sentences.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Now he wants to take that plea back and claims actual innocence.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.900
To understand how we got to this massive reversal, we need to look at what the evidence actually shows from the very beginning.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.000
The record starts with the timeline of that frigid November night.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.900
At 1:45 a.m., Xana and Ethan returned to the King Road house.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.500
Eleven minutes later, Madison and Kaylee got a ride home from a private individual.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:04.300
At 4:00 a.m., Xana received a DoorDash order, and phone records show she was using the TikTok app twelve minutes after that.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:11.800
Detectives believe all four murders occurred between 4:00 and 4:25 a.m. Madison and Kaylee were killed in a bedroom on the third floor.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.100
Xana and Ethan were murdered in a bedroom on the second floor.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.900
Investigators believe the killer used a fixed-blade KA-BAR knife.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.000
That weapon has never been recovered.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.400
When a massive case like this breaks, the public reaction is always a major part of the story.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.400
The limited flow of information from authorities frustrated a lot of people.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:38.400
The fathers of Kaylee Goncalves and Ethan Chapin openly criticized the lack of communication from police and the university to the victims' families.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.100
The fear on campus was immediate.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.800
The university canceled classes for November 14.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.500
From the very day of the killings, investigators initially told the public there was no risk to the community.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.600
But just three days later, Moscow Police Chief James Fry had to reverse course.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.700
He stood in front of the press and stated they could not say there was no threat.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.400
That sudden shift completely fractured the community's trust in those early assurances.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.500
Fall break was scheduled to begin after November 18, with classes resuming ten days later.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.000
But students and Moscow residents were terrified.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.800
They started an early Thanksgiving holiday exodus just to get out of the area.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.700
The people who stayed behind were incredibly anxious, and some professors simply canceled their remaining classes.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:24.000
The university tried to organize a candlelight vigil on the administration building lawn for November 16.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.100
They ended up postponing it for two weeks.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:32.000
Due to weather concerns, grieving families and classmates finally gathered indoors at the Kibbie Dome on November 30.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.300
Ethan's mother, Stacy Chapin, cried as she told the mourners that time is precious and you cannot get it back.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:43.100
Kaylee's dad, Steve Goncalves, spoke about how his daughter and Madison had been inseparable since the sixth grade.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:48.900
He shared the heartbreaking detail that the two lifelong best friends died together in the same room, in the same bed.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.200
Let's unpack the staggering administrative details of the investigation.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.500
The Moscow Police Department didn't work this massive case alone.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.000
They relied heavily on support from the Idaho State Police and the FBI.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
Nearly one hundred and thirty law enforcement personnel were assigned to work on the homicides.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.100
The sheer volume of public input they had to manage was unprecedented for the small department.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:15.400
Investigators set up dedicated phone lines and email addresses through the FBI just to handle the influx.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:21.300
By early December, they had already logged over two thousand six hundred emails and twenty-seven hundred phone calls.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.600
They also processed a thousand digital media submissions from people trying to help.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.700
The tips just kept pouring in as the weeks went on.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.700
On December 24, investigators reported receiving over fifteen thousand tips regarding the case.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.500
Every single one of those leads had to be cataloged, assigned, and tracked by that task force.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.800
Investigators were chasing down everything, even early rumors.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.900
For example, the police chief had previously mentioned receiving tips that Kaylee had a stalker.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:51.400
Investigators chased that down but could not verify the claim or identify any such individual.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.900
They also initially left open the possibility of multiple perpetrators.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:02.000
Early on, police stated they believed it was a targeted attack, but they hadn't concluded if the target was the house itself or the specific occupants.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.600
That information vacuum was quickly filled by the internet.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.200
TikTokers, self-proclaimed psychics, and social media users began to speculate wildly.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.900
Rampant rumors and misinformation started spreading online as people tried to solve the case from their couches.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.300
The Moscow Police Department had to step in and push back against the internet sleuths.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.300
They criticized these online investigators for creating a huge distraction.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:32.700
Police warned that spreading false facts and speculation without factual backing was stoking community fears and threatening the integrity of the investigation.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:41.900
Moscow Police Captain Roger Lanier publicly stated that tracking down and quelling rumors about events that never even happened was pulling resources away from the actual manhunt.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:47.100
He explained they were keeping specific details quiet because they did not want to compromise the case.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.600
The department even had to issue a formal warning.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:56.400
They made it clear that people harassing or threatening those potentially involved with the case could face actual criminal charges.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.800
The legal battle started before the ink on the charging documents was even dry.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:08.800
The case file shows Pennsylvania State Police and the FBI coordinated a pre-dawn raid at his parents' home in Albrightsville, where he had traveled for winter break.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.500
Investigators executed search warrants and seized personal electronics, clothing, and the white Hyundai Elantra.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.900
After his arrest in Pennsylvania, he was held at the Monroe County Correctional Facility.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.600
Kohberger waived his right to extradition on January 3.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.100
He was flown back to Idaho the very next day and remanded to the Latah County jail without bail.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:32.200
A judge quickly issued a strict gag order that barred attorneys and law enforcement from discussing the case with the public.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.500
Kohberger was appointed a public defender to navigate what was shaping up to be a massive capital murder trial.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.300
His defense team, which included attorney Anne Taylor, immediately went on the offensive.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:53.100
They filed a wave of pretrial motions asking the court to dismiss the indictment, to suppress the biological evidence, and to challenge the state's use of investigative genetic genealogy.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:59.000
Before the arrest, authorities had collected trash from the Pennsylvania home and sent it to the Idaho State Lab.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:04.800
That trash yielded a familial DNA match to Kohberger's father, which investigators used to secure the warrant.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:11.300
By May 2023, a grand jury indicted Kohberger on four counts of first-degree murder and one count of felony burglary.

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.100
Prosecutors built their case on more than just the DNA on the knife sheath.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:21.800
Surveillance footage showed a white Hyundai Elantra driving near the house multiple times during the exact window of the attacks.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:32.100
Cell network records also indicated Kohberger's phone registered near the residence at least twelve times in the months before the murders, though the signal vanished during the hours of the slayings.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.400
At his arraignment, he chose to stand silent.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.300
This prompted the presiding judge to enter a plea of not guilty on his behalf.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:43.900
On June 26, the Latah County Prosecutor's office officially declared their intent to seek the death penalty.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:49.700
Prosecutors cited the aggravating circumstances of the murders to justify the ultimate punishment.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.200
That decision raised the stakes of the upcoming trial significantly.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.900
It was a full-blown capital case.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.600
For a small college town that had not seen a single murder since 2015, the pressure was immense.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.800
Even with the charges filed, a motive for the crimes remained completely unknown.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:09.100
As they prepared for trial, his attorneys offered an alternate explanation for his whereabouts.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:13.200
They stated that Kohberger was simply out driving alone on the night of the killings.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.200
The defense also successfully pushed to transfer the case to Ada County due to intense local publicity.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:24.100
On September 9, the Idaho Supreme Court officially moved the trial to Boise and assigned Judge Steven Hippler to take over.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:27.100
Kohberger was flown south and booked into the Ada County jail.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.300
The battle lines for a death penalty trial were officially drawn.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:35.200
The calendar just kept shifting as both sides argued over the rules of engagement.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.500
A preliminary probable cause hearing was originally put on the docket for June 26.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:44.900
That hearing ended up being canceled entirely once the grand jury handed down their indictment in May.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:52.900
That indictment included the four murder charges plus one count of felony burglary, which meant breaking into a home with the intent to commit a felony.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:59.900
As the motions piled up, the judge tentatively set jury selection to start on August 4, 2025, with the trial beginning two weeks later.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.300
The defense team knew the touch DNA on that tan leather sheath was the state's most powerful weapon.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.900
They made it their primary target.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.000
They didn't just question the swab itself.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.400
They went after the entire method investigators used to put a name to that genetic profile.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.300
The arguments centered entirely around the use of investigative genetic genealogy.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:23.600
The record shows local police originally handed the DNA sample to Othram, a private forensic company.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.500
Othram started building family trees, but they hit a wall.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:31.200
The closest match they found shared just over seventy centimorgans of DNA with the suspect.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.800
That is the genetic equivalent of sharing a great-great-grandparent.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:41.600
The company suggested contacting potential relatives to narrow the pool, but the FBI stepped in and took over the search completely.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.500
That handoff became the cornerstone of the defense's challenge.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:50.800
To find a closer match, the FBI ran the profile through ancestral websites like GEDmatch and MyHeritage.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.300
The Bureau later acknowledged those searches included users who had not opted in to law enforcement access.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:02.100
The defense pointed out this went directly against a Justice Department policy regarding explicit user consent.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.000
But the controversial search worked.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.900
The FBI found a relative sharing two hundred and fifty centimorgans of DNA, drastically narrowing the suspect pool.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.700
The family tree data just gave them a name to look at, which led them to the trash outside the Pennsylvania home.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.500
The actual legal proof came even later.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:23.200
Later samples taken from Kohberger after he was apprehended provided a statistical match to the DNA on the knife sheath.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:30.300
With the DNA battle raging, intense scrutiny shifted to the cellular network records to track the accused's movements.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:36.500
Cell phone data shows his phone stopped connecting to the network in Pullman at 2:47 a.m. on the night of the murders.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.900
It did not reconnect until 4:48 a.m. near Blaine, Idaho, which sits south of Moscow.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:47.700
Investigators argue the device was intentionally turned off or put into airplane mode during the exact window of the attacks.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.100
But the timeline didn't just focus on that single night.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:58.500
Historical location data indicated the phone pinged from the tower nearest the residence at least twelve times between June 2022 and November 13.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.800
There was one more digital detail.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:07.300
Cell phone data showed the phone connected to a cell tower near the victims' residence again at roughly 9:00 a.m. on November 13.

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:12.900
That is about five hours after the slayings, but still hours before the surviving roommates ever called 911.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:18.900
The court calendar was locked for a massive capital trial, with jury selection set for August 2025.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:24.100
Both sides had spent years arguing over cellular data, genetic genealogy, and police tactics.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.900
Then, on June 30, everything came to an abrupt halt.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:30.700
An announcement confirmed a sudden shift in the legal proceedings.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.200
The prosecution and the defense had reached a comprehensive plea agreement behind closed doors.

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.600
Kohberger agreed to plead guilty to all five charges against him.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.700
That meant accepting the four counts of first-degree murder and the single count of felony burglary.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:47.300
In exchange, the death penalty came off the table.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:51.700
The deal was specifically structured for Kohberger to avoid the death penalty.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:56.100
State prosecutors formally agreed to withdraw capital punishment from the table entirely.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:12:01.300
The terms dictated that he would accept four consecutive life sentences without the possibility of parole.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:05.000
He also agreed to serve an additional ten years for the burglary conviction.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:09.800
To finalize this arrangement, Kohberger had to waive his right to any future appeals.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:13.100
The agreement guaranteed he would remain in prison until he dies.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:18.200
Two days later, on July 2, Kohberger walked into the Ada County Courthouse to make it official.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.600
The judge still needed to formally accept the terms and conduct the change of plea hearing.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:24.700
We know exactly what happened in that room.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.600
For the very first time, the defendant publicly admitted to the four killings.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:33.600
Judge Hippler looked directly at him and asked if he was pleading guilty because he was actually guilty.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.800
When prompted by the court, Kohberger officially entered his guilty plea.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:43.100
He confirmed out loud that he was waiving his right to a trial and giving up all future appeals to secure the deal.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:48.200
All the previous battles over cellular data and forensic evidence were suddenly in the rearview mirror.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:51.600
He pleaded guilty to all four counts of first-degree murder.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.700
That meant formally admitting to the fatal stabbings of the four students on the record.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:13:01.800
There would be no jury selection, no cross-examination, and no need for the state to present their timeline in front of a gallery.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.200
The judge then moved to the final charge listed on the grand jury indictment.

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:09.800
Kohberger also pleaded guilty to the single count of felony burglary.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:17.200
By legally admitting he entered that off-campus rental house with the intent to commit a crime, he cemented the final piece of the state's case.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.000
The plea was officially accepted.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:23.400
Judge Hippler then formally scheduled the sentencing hearing for July 23 at 9:00 a.m.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:29.400
Immediately following the hearing, the emotional toll of the morning spilled out onto the courthouse steps.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:33.700
Attorney Leander James issued a public statement on behalf of Madison Mogen's family.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:38.900
He told the press that the family was turning from tragedy and the dark uncertainty of the legal process.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:43.200
James stated they finally had closure and were embarking on a path of hope and healing.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.900
At that moment, the case appeared completely resolved.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:52.100
The calendar flipped to July 23, 2025, the date Judge Hippler had set aside to finalize the punishment.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:58.600
The families of Ethan, Madison, Xana, and Kaylee gathered inside the Ada County Courthouse for the sentencing phase.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:04.200
You have to remember that for years, this case was dominated by pretrial motions and forensic debates.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.700
Now, the legal arguments over cell towers and DNA were finished.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:11.100
The focus of the proceedings shifted entirely to the victims.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.600
The judge dedicated a full, day-long hearing strictly for the families to speak.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.500
One by one, relatives took the podium to deliver their victim impact statements.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.600
They stood just feet away from the defendant to put their pain on the official record.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:28.100
The courtroom listened as they described the devastating reality of losing their children.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.600
These statements are a crucial part of the justice system.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:37.200
They ensure the human cost of a crime is documented forever, separate from the sterile facts of an indictment.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.200
The families spoke about the bright futures of four college students that were violently cut short.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:44.600
Madison and Xana were both studying marketing.

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:47.800
Kaylee had just moved out and was only visiting for the weekend.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.600
Ethan was just twenty years old, and the girls were twenty and twenty-one.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:56.900
The record shows a sign hanging in the window of their rental house simply read, Saturday is for the girls.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:01.400
They detailed the massive void left in their own lives and the broader Moscow community.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:06.900
For hours, the gallery heard exactly what was taken from the world in that brief window on King Road.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:11.400
The loved ones of the victims made sure the court understood the true weight of the tragedy.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:16.500
It was a grueling, emotional day that brought the human element of this massive case into sharp focus.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:21.700
When the statements finally concluded, Judge Hippler officially imposed the sentence on the record.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:27.900
He handed down the four consecutive life terms in prison without the possibility of parole, plus the ten years for burglary.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:34.700
The court also ordered a combined fine of two hundred and fifty thousand dollars, which breaks down to fifty thousand per count.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:39.800
And he was ordered to pay twenty thousand dollars in restitution, five thousand dollars for each victim.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:43.500
The judge formally handed down four consecutive life terms.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:47.800
That specific structure is incredibly important in a case with multiple victims.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:52.000
It means the punishment for each student's murder must be served back-to-back.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.000
The court ensured the penalty reflected four distinct and separate crimes.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:16:00.400
This sentence ensures imprisonment without the possibility of parole.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.300
There is no future date where a review board will ever evaluate his release.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:07.700
He is incarcerated at the Idaho Maximum Security Institution.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:12.700
Under the strict terms accepted by the court, he will remain behind bars for the rest of his life.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:19.800
Before his arrest, he was a twenty-eight-year-old criminology student completing his first semester at Washington State University.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.200
He lived in Pullman, less than eight miles away from the King Road house.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:27.500
Now, at thirty-one years old, his reality is completely confined to a state penitentiary.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:31.600
The transition from a university classroom to a maximum-security cell was absolute.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.300
The plea agreement seemed entirely ironclad.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:39.800
The state had officially withdrawn the death penalty, and in exchange, Kohberger explicitly waived his right to appeal.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:43.500
You might think a signed waiver means the legal fight is over.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:44.100
It doesn't.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:50.900
Waiving an appeal does not legally prevent a defendant from filing a new case to challenge the validity of the plea agreement itself.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:56.000
In July 2026, Kohberger filed a petition for post-conviction relief in Ada County District Court.

00:16:56.500 --> 00:17:01.300
The handwritten court documents outline his specific complaints against his former defense team.

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:07.400
Citing ineffective assistance of counsel, Kohberger claims his attorneys failed to disclose or review exculpatory evidence.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:13.500
He also wrote that he was given a gross misrepresentation of what a life sentence and death row in Idaho would actually look like.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.500
Anne Taylor's office declined to comment on the accusations.

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:22.200
The New York Times first reported on this petition after conducting an interview with Kohberger in prison.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:26.200
In a statement provided to the paper, he claimed he was convinced to falsely confess.

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:32.100
He stated his actual innocence is his truth, and argued the plea was hinged on false promises and blatant disinformation.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.100
The state is not backing down.

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:40.800
Officials and legal counsel representing the victims' families publicly affirmed their confidence in the original conviction.

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:44.300
They stated their readiness to challenge the new petition in court.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:50.900
Meanwhile, on August 29, 2026, Kohberger formally asked the court to disqualify Judge Hippler from presiding over the new case.

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:54.500
The timing of this reversal was impossible to ignore.

00:17:54.500 --> 00:18:02.000
He publicly revealed his petition to rescind the plea the exact same week a major documentary about the murders premiered on Netflix.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:07.200
That project, titled "The Idaho Murders: College Nightmare," was set to release on July 29.

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.500
The media spotlight on this case is only getting brighter.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:18.800
A new book by Christopher Whitcomb titled "Broken Plea: The Explosive Search for Truth Behind the Idaho Murders" is set to be published on April 28.

